The issue is happening because inside your handleOpenViewModal function, you have explicit state resets, but when a user clicks **"Back to Details"**, it triggers setIsActionMode(false) without wiping the sub-states, *except* that handleOpenViewModal isn't called again. However, if the modal re-renders or checks status configurations, it re-reads from vidDetails which defaults back to standard parameters.
To ensure **all scheduling choices** (scheduleOverrideType and finalScheduleTime) are safely preserved exactly as the user selected them when toggling back and forth between screens, we just need to prevent any implicit state resets and make sure we don't accidentally wipe them out.
### The Fix
Look at your **"Back to Details"** button configuration inside the bottom sticky action bar (around line 908):
```javascript
<Button onClick={() => setIsActionMode(false)} sx={jaStyles.backBtn} disabled={isSubmitting}>
  Back to Details
</Button>

```
The state is preserved here perfectly because clicking this only sets isActionMode to false. The problem is that when you click **"Proceed to Action"** again, the handleProceed function runs and **overwrites** your choices back to defaults!
Let's fix handleProceed so it checks if a user has *already* made an override modification before resetting it.
**Find your existing handleProceed function (around line 431):**
```javascript
  const handleProceed = () => {

    setIsActionMode(true); // Open the universal action screen

    if (viewingBatch?.isFinalApproval === "Y") {
      const isLapsed = vidDetails?.SCHEDULED_TIME && new Date(vidDetails.SCHEDULED_TIME) < new Date();

      if (isLapsed) {
        setScheduleOverrideType(null); // DO NOT auto-select override
        setFinalScheduleTime(""); // Leave time blank
      } else {
        setScheduleOverrideType("KEEP"); // Default behavior if time hasn't lapsed
        if (vidDetails?.SCHEDULED_TIME) {
          setFinalScheduleTime(dayjs(vidDetails.SCHEDULED_TIME).format("YYYY-MM-DDTHH:mm"));
        } else {
          setFinalScheduleTime("");
        }
      }
    }
  };

```
**Replace it with this smart checking version:**
```javascript
  const handleProceed = () => {
    setIsActionMode(true); // Open the universal action screen

    if (viewingBatch?.isFinalApproval === "Y") {
      // --- THE FIX: Only set defaults if the user hasn't touched the scheduling preferences yet ---
      if (scheduleOverrideType === "KEEP" && !finalScheduleTime) {
        const isLapsed = vidDetails?.SCHEDULED_TIME && new Date(vidDetails.SCHEDULED_TIME) < new Date();

        if (isLapsed) {
          setScheduleOverrideType(null); 
          setFinalScheduleTime(""); 
        } else {
          setScheduleOverrideType("KEEP"); 
          if (vidDetails?.SCHEDULED_TIME) {
            setFinalScheduleTime(dayjs(vidDetails.SCHEDULED_TIME).format("YYYY-MM-DDTHH:mm"));
          } else {
            setFinalScheduleTime("");
          }
        }
      }
      // If scheduleOverrideType is already "OVERRIDE" or has values changed by user, we do NOTHING.
      // This leaves their choices completely untouched when they come back!
    }
  };

```
### Why this solves it:
When the user configures an intermediate schedule or selects an explicit override option, changes their mind, clicks **"Back to Details"**, and then clicks **"Proceed to Action"** again, the updated condition realizes that scheduleOverrideType or finalScheduleTime are already customized, skipping the initialization block entirely. Your workflow choices will now persist flawlessly between screen shifts.
